95	// Oracle exit-drain mode (verify_oracle F1): when exit_marker_mode or
96	// exit_pc_mode is set (plugin args `exit_marker=1` / `exit_pc=0x...`), a
97	// pure-QEMU run produces the differential verifier's reference state. Every
98	// insn gets an exec callback: the tick ARMS on the
99	// ROI exit boundary (EXIT marker match, or vaddr == exit_pc) and DRAINS on
100	// the next callback. Exec callbacks fire before their insn executes, so the
101	// armed drain captures "after the boundary insn retired" -- exactly vsim's
102	// exit-drain record (DPC = next insn to execute) -- and reports that next
103	// insn's vaddr as pc, correct even when the boundary insn branches.
104	// Exclusive with the other trigger modes: the oracle run never hands off.
105	//
106	// drain_pc=0x... (optional modifier): drain at the first post-arm tick whose
107	// vaddr equals drain_pc, instead of the very next tick. The differential
108	// harness passes vsim's actual drained pc here: vsim's DMI halt lands a
109	// variable 0-3 insns past the trigger (the core keeps retiring while the
110	// haltreq propagates), and the two states only compare equal when both
111	// sides sample the SAME architectural point. If vsim's pc is never reached
112	// (a real control-flow divergence), the oracle never drains and QEMU runs
113	// to completion -- the caller sees exit != 200.
